PR Title: feat: JWT Decoder

The Problem Solved: Added a robust developer utility to decode JSON Web Tokens, parse the header and payload, and provide a helpful visual indicator if the token has expired based on the `exp` claim.

Visuals:
- Screenshot: [preview_scroll.png](/home/jules/verification/screenshots/preview_scroll.png)
- Video: [50c553e5ef62c16eb4075ee716087af5.webm](/home/jules/verification/videos/50c553e5ef62c16eb4075ee716087af5.webm)

Implementation Journey:
- Verified remote branches to ensure no duplicate work.
- Created `JwtDecoder` component to handle parsing, Base64Url decoding, and expiration logic.
- Wrote thorough unit tests covering valid, invalid, expired, and future-dated tokens.
- Integrated the component into the main application layout and Side Navigation.
- Verified the implementation visually using Playwright automation.
- Cleaned up git staging area and committed changes.

Tradeoffs & Assumptions:
- I assumed the user wanted a net-new feature, so I avoided duplicating existing remote branches.
- I considered three paths: Standard (input + output fields), Minimalist (paste and go), and Lateral (signature validation). I chose a Minimalist approach with added Expiration checking, as this provides the highest developer value for quick debugging.
- I assumed `base64url` formatting (replacing `-` and `_`) was necessary since JWTs use this encoding, and implemented a robust replace before `atob`.

Testing Instructions:
1. Pull the branch `feature/jwt-decoder`.
2. Run `npm install` and `npm start`.
3. Open `http://localhost:3000/quick` and navigate to the "JWT Decoder" section.
4. Paste a valid JWT into the input field to view the decoded header and payload, and observe the expiration status.

Action Item: `git push -u origin feature/jwt-decoder` (simulated via submit tool)
